---
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../PhotoPlaceholder.astro';
import VisionFigure from '../VisionFigure.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section data-vision-section class="band-ink section">
  <SectionHead heading="We didn't move here for the traffic." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <PhotoPlaceholder
      variant="ink"
      description="Red Rock Canyon at sunset, or a packed downtown First Friday — a Vegas weekend that isn't on the Strip. People doing what they actually moved here for."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        Most people who live in the Las&nbsp;Vegas Valley moved here for something. The mountains.
        The summers, somehow. The desert. The Strip. The cost of living. The lack of state income
        tax. None of those reasons are <em
          >"I love driving forty minutes to a trailhead with a parking lot at the end."</em
        > But that is the experience the Valley currently offers for almost any recreation that isn't
        on the Strip itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-page">
    <VisionFigure num="10" title="A Valley you can leave the house for">
      <svg
        class="rec-map"
        viewBox="0 0 600 360"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Map of Valley recreation destinations: Red Rock, Mount Charleston, Lake Mead, Floyd Lamb Park, Springs Preserve, downtown, the Strip with Allegiant and T-Mobile arenas, Henderson trails"
      >
        <path
          class="rec-bound"
          d="M80 200 Q110 120, 200 90 Q320 70, 440 95 Q520 130, 530 220 Q525 290, 460 320 Q360 340, 250 330 Q140 320, 95 270 Z"
        ></path>
        <text class="rec-axis" x="80" y="80">Spring Mts</text>
        <text class="rec-axis" x="500" y="120">Lake Mead Rec Area</text>
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="80" cy="190" r="6"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="92"
          y="194">Red Rock</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="60" cy="100" r="6"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="72"
          y="104">Mt. Charleston</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="510" cy="160" r="6"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="498"
          y="148"
          text-anchor="end">Lake Mead</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="240" cy="120" r="5"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="240"
          y="108"
          text-anchor="middle">Floyd Lamb Park</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="320" cy="160" r="5"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="320"
          y="148"
          text-anchor="middle">Springs Preserve</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="290" cy="200" r="6"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="290"
          y="222"
          text-anchor="middle">Downtown</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="320" cy="240" r="6"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="320"
          y="262"
          text-anchor="middle">Strip · Allegiant · T-Mobile</text
        >
        <circle class="rec-dot" cx="450" cy="290" r="5"></circle><text
          class="rec-label"
          x="450"
          y="312"
          text-anchor="middle">Henderson trails</text
        >
      </svg>
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        The Valley is full of places people moved here for. Most of them are unreachable today
        without a car. The won Valley closes that gap.
      </Fragment>
    </VisionFigure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        Red Rock Canyon, Mount Charleston, Lake Mead, Floyd Lamb Park, the Springs Preserve,
        downtown's First Friday, the hockey games at T-Mobile, the football games at Allegiant —
        these are the things people moved here for, and they are all currently locked behind a car
        ride. The won Valley unlocks them — not with light rail to a trailhead, but with transit to
        a station where a shuttle or a bike-share covers the rest, biking distance to downtown
        culture, and a real shuttle network from BRT stops to the regional parks.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Valley you can actually play in is a Valley worth growing up in. And it's a Valley people
        keep choosing to move to — which is something Las Vegas has always wanted to be able to
        count on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .rec-map {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 360px;
  }
  .rec-bound {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 4%, transparent);
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 24%, transparent);
    stroke-width: 1;
    stroke-dasharray: 4 5;
  }
  .rec-dot {
    fill: var(--color-primary);
  }
  .rec-label {
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 700;
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .rec-axis {
    font-size: 9px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--vp-text-muted);
  }
</style>
